The continuous random variable $X$ has probability density function $f(x)=\cos x$ on $\left[\frac{3\pi}{2},2\pi\right]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=left, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=4.4, xmax=6.6, ymin=0, ymax=1.3, samples=100, clip=false, width=8cm, height=6cm, xtick={4.7124,6.2832}, xticklabels={$\frac{3\pi}{2}$,$2\pi$}, ytick={0.5,1}]
\addplot[thick, domain=4.7124:6.2832] {cos(deg(x))};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the mode of $X$.

questions.solution_text:
$f'(x)=-\sin x$
$\sin x<0$ for $\frac{3\pi}{2}<x<2\pi$, so $f'(x)>0$ across the support.
The density increases all the way to the right endpoint.
$f\left(\frac{3\pi}{2}\right)=0$ and $f(2\pi)=1$.
The mode is $2\pi$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=left, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=4.4, xmax=6.6, ymin=0, ymax=1.3, samples=100, clip=false, width=8cm, height=6cm, xtick={4.7124,6.2832}, xticklabels={$\frac{3\pi}{2}$,$2\pi$}, ytick={0.5,1}]
\addplot[thick, domain=4.7124:6.2832] {cos(deg(x))};
\draw[dashed] (axis cs:6.2832,0) -- (axis cs:6.2832,1);
\node[above left] at (axis cs:6.2832,1.02) {mode $=2\pi$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

